"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,040\nThe Learning Brain.\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,506\nEpisode two.\n\n3\n00:00:07,526 --> 00:00:09,041\nAttention.\n\n4\n00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:14,720\nImagine a desk with four objects on it.\n\n5\n00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:18,213\nLight from all four reaches your eyes at once.\n\n6\n00:00:18,449 --> 00:00:21,858\nBut your brain can't fully process all of them at once.\n\n7\n00:00:22,041 --> 00:00:24,781\nSo how does it decide which one gets through?\n\n8\n00:00:24,851 --> 00:00:26,556\nStart at the back of the brain,\n\n9\n00:00:26,576 --> 00:00:27,976\nin the visual cortex.\n\n10\n00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:31,546\nEach cell there watches one small patch of the scene.\n\n11\n00:00:31,837 --> 00:00:34,440\nThat patch is called its receptive field.\n\n12\n00:00:34,510 --> 00:00:35,840\nIn the first area,\n\n13\n00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:36,009\nV1,\n\n14\n00:00:36,029 --> 00:00:37,831\nthe patches are tiny.\n\n15\n00:00:37,995 --> 00:00:40,246\nAbout one and a half degrees across.\n\n16\n00:00:40,487 --> 00:00:42,345\nAs you move forward,\n\n17\n00:00:42,505 --> 00:00:43,089\nthey grow.\n\n18\n00:00:43,109 --> 00:00:44,456\nFour degrees in V4.\n\n19\n00:00:44,589 --> 00:00:46,822\nTwenty-six by the last stage.\n\n20\n00:00:46,892 --> 00:00:48,512\nAnd that creates a problem.\n\n21\n00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:51,804\nA big patch can hold several objects at once.\n\n22\n00:00:52,053 --> 00:00:52,993\nWhen it does,\n\n23\n00:00:53,013 --> 00:00:55,486\nthe cell doesn't add their signals together.\n\n24\n00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,091\nThey suppress each other.\n\n25\n00:00:57,161 --> 00:00:58,465\nYou can measure this.\n\n26\n00:00:58,678 --> 00:01:00,497\nShow four images one at a time,\n\n27\n00:01:00,517 --> 00:01:01,806\nthen all together.\n\n28\n00:01:02,031 --> 00:01:02,378\nIn V4,\n\n29\n00:01:02,398 --> 00:01:05,308\nthe response to all together is smaller.\n\n30\n00:01:05,633 --> 00:01:06,696\nIn V1,\n\n31\n00:01:06,717 --> 00:01:09,786\nwhere the patches are too small to hold more than one,\n\n32\n00:01:09,856 --> 00:01:11,700\nalmost nothing changes.\n\n33\n00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:13,305\nSo objects compete.\n\n34\n00:01:13,517 --> 00:01:15,788\nThe question is what tips the balance.\n\n35\n00:01:15,993 --> 00:01:18,527\nOne answer comes from the scene itself.\n\n36\n00:01:18,757 --> 00:01:20,078\nA thing that's brighter,\n\n37\n00:01:20,098 --> 00:01:22,626\nor just different from everything around it,\n\n38\n00:01:22,646 --> 00:01:23,969\nwins automatically.\n\n39\n00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:25,344\nThat's bottom-up.\n\n40\n00:01:25,414 --> 00:01:27,177\nThe other answer comes from you.\n\n41\n00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,919\nAreas in the frontal and parietal cortex send a signal back down,\n\n42\n00:01:31,987 --> 00:01:34,399\ntoward the cells for the place you choose.\n\n43\n00:01:34,629 --> 00:01:36,013\nThat's top-down.\n\n44\n00:01:36,083 --> 00:01:37,521\nIt does two things.\n\n45\n00:01:37,709 --> 00:01:40,341\nIt boosts the response to what you chose.\n\n46\n00:01:40,478 --> 00:01:43,498\nAnd it cancels much of the suppression from its neighbors.\n\n47\n00:01:43,568 --> 00:01:46,330\nIt even shows up before anything appears.\n\n48\n00:01:46,609 --> 00:01:47,622\nIn monkeys,\n\n49\n00:01:47,736 --> 00:01:52,520\ncells covering an expected location fire thirty to forty percent more,\n\n50\n00:01:52,664 --> 00:01:54,436\nwhile the screen is still blank.\n\n51\n00:01:54,506 --> 00:01:55,653\nOne caution.\n\n52\n00:01:55,946 --> 00:01:58,962\nMost of this evidence comes from simple displays,\n\n53\n00:01:58,982 --> 00:02:00,248\nbrain scans,\n\n54\n00:02:00,268 --> 00:02:02,066\nand recordings in monkeys.\n\n55\n00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:07,379\nHow cleanly it carries over to a crowded lecture slide is an open question.\n\n56\n00:02:07,449 --> 00:02:07,837\nStill,\n\n57\n00:02:07,857 --> 00:02:09,677\nit suggests two habits.\n\n58\n00:02:09,917 --> 00:02:12,749\nTell students where to look before the slide appears,\n\n59\n00:02:12,769 --> 00:02:14,948\nso the bias is in place early.\n\n60\n00:02:15,209 --> 00:02:18,257\nAnd cut what competes with the one thing that matters.\n\n61\n00:02:18,327 --> 00:02:20,469\nSo attention is not one thing.\n\n62\n00:02:20,685 --> 00:02:21,779\nIt's a bias,\n\n63\n00:02:21,816 --> 00:02:24,131\non a competition that's already happening.\n\n64\n00:02:24,414 --> 00:02:27,879\nAnd whatever wins gets passed on to memory and action.\n\n65\n00:02:28,175 --> 00:02:30,033\nThat's biased competition.\n"
